Brandon - the deal is that some of the observations are replicated and it's often the case that one but not all of the replicates is censored. The idea was that, in the case where replicates were present with censoring, I'd use the cenmle or cenros procedure to get summary statistics that account for censoring. That part of it wasn't finished when I posted because I knew some pre-processing was needed for calling the NADA procedures but I couldn't even get acast to call my procedure properly with the data I needed. That's what prompted the query. So, it's a question of selecting the data like acast does, sending the selections to cmean, figuring out what to do, and then returning the summary statistics to put in the table that acast creates. There may be (usually is) a better way to do all this but I've used acast a bit with R/stats and other procedures with success, so I was starting there.
So, once I get acast (or whatever) to successfully call my procedure, I can move forward from there.

